>
> The idea is to have a dangerous item in each (meaningful) pricegroup.
> Destroy armor is in the same group as fire, so that might be considered
> redundant.
>
> But How can it be "too evil" when reading un-IDed scrolls is such a stupid
> thing to do anyway? (I tend towards the opinion that it's meaningless).

It's too evil for the newbie, who doesn't yet have the ironclad habit of
"don't read unIDed scrolls" and thus gets his armor flushed and dies.

It's meaningless because once you realize that reading unIDed scrolls is
never a good option (confirmable price-ID counts as IDed), the scroll
ends up as nothing more than a candidate for blanking.

(By creating things like this, Nethack perpetuated its own binary problem: 
some scrolls are extremely dangerous, and no scrolls are comparably
beneficial, so the player must never read unIDed scrolls.  Contrast with
engrave-testing wands; the most dangerous one is "create monster", and
the best is wishing, so the only reason to ever spend effort identifying
wands formally is to find out the exact number of charges, and you
should always engrave-test wands ASAP after picking one up.  Note how
both classes of objects have an "always do" approach.)

If the reason for its existence is "preventing the user from price-IDing 
scrolls", there are better, less complex ways to perform that function.

